# THE COFFEE STORE'S VITRINE, ON THE BENCH EDGE.
#
# `caddy/Caddyfile.local` ends with `import /etc/caddy/extra/*.local.caddy`, and this is the file that hole
# was left for. It adds a front the committed edge does not know about, and it edits nothing.
#
# ── WHY A PATH AND NOT A HOSTNAME ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# On this bench a store is reached at `/s/<handle>` (host -> store is DATA, and the bench has one origin).
# The coffee store's handle is `cafe`, so everything under `/s/cafe` is this shop and goes to its own
# container; every other store on the box keeps falling through to the reference vitrine. In a real
# deployment the same block is written against `{$FORGE_COFFEE_DOMAIN}` instead, and the container does not
# change: the front resolves its store from the request either way.
#
# ⚠️ SPECIFICITY, NOT ORDER. Caddy matches `handle` blocks by how SPECIFIC the path is, wherever they appear
# — so this wins over `Caddyfile.local`'s bare fall-through no matter which file is read first. It is written
# here because that is where the seam is, not because position decides anything.
#
# ⚠️ AND WHAT IS DELIBERATELY *NOT* ROUTED HERE — this is the part that fails silently if got wrong:
#
#   · `/checkout*`, `/account*`, `/_checkout/*`, `/apps/*` — the CHECKOUT's, and more specific than this
#     block, so they already win. Written down anyway because "why does the coffee checkout look like the
#     vanilla one" has exactly one right answer: it is supposed to. It wears the THEME, not the fork.
handle /s/cafe* {
	reverse_proxy storefront-coffee:3000
}

# ★★ THE ASSETS, AND THIS IS THE BLOCK THAT FAILS IN SILENCE IF IT IS WRONG.
#
# Splitting the vitrines by PATH does nothing for an asset URL, because an asset URL starts at the ROOT:
# every Next app on this origin asks the browser for `/_next/static/…` by default, and the edge can send
# that to only one container. The other one then loads chunks from a server that never emitted them — and
# nothing anywhere says so. The HTML arrives, no server logs a thing, and the shop is UNSTYLED. This front
# therefore sets `assetPrefix: '/_coffee'` (storefront-coffee/next.config.mjs) and this block is the half at
# the edge.
#
# ⚠️⚠️ `handle_path`, NOT `handle` — six characters and the whole thing. `handle_path` STRIPS the matched
# prefix before proxying. MEASURED on the real image in CHECKOUT-APP C3: a Next server with an `assetPrefix`
# EMITS the prefixed URL into its HTML and then answers **404** to it — it serves those files at
# `/_next/static/…` and nowhere else. The prefix is an instruction to THIS FILE, not a route the app owns.
# With a bare `handle` every chunk reaches the right container and comes back 404, which is the same
# unstyled shop the prefix exists to prevent, one layer further in.
handle_path /_coffee/* {
	reverse_proxy storefront-coffee:3000
}
